Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

From an adversarial audit of the implementation against the proposal
(notes/audit-report.md, 11 verified defects; ledger section E).

MF11 (blocking): 'throws' and 'captures' were reserved keywords whenever the
feature was enabled, so any existing identifier with those names failed to parse
— 'std.unittest.mock' exports a public 'throws' function used at hundreds of
call sites, which would have broken the stdlib migration at parse time.
Proposal 10 requires soft keywords: both are now lexed as ordinary identifiers
and recognised by spelling only at the seven positions where a clause may start.
The class-header parser probes the clause before its "expected '{' or '<'"
recovery, which would otherwise swallow it.

MF5: a capturing class supplied its captured capabilities to static members,
finalizers and static field initializers — none of which has a receiver to carry
one, and all of which proposal 3.9 rule 1 forbids checked throws in. Silent
escape; now gated on the body having a receiver.

MF7: capability parameter inference never saw the enclosing class's 'captures',
because that supply was owned by the member-walking entry point that inference
bypasses. Every eligible member of a capturing class collected the captured
requirement as residual and charged it to its callers — exactly the burden
'captures' exists to remove. Captures are now derived from the declaration's
owner, so inference and reporting see identical supplies.

MF10: the polymorphic-recursion check ran during call-graph construction rather
than per cycle, so it rejected ordinary generic code (a non-recursive 'wrap<T>'
calling 'sink<Array<T>>') while being structurally unable to fire on its own
motivating case, since self-calls are not graph edges. It now runs per strongly
connected component, includes self-calls, and skips calls that leave the cycle.

Gate: CheckedExceptions 50/58 (the 8 failures are the three features not yet
implemented); ctest 49/49; Parser/Sema LLT 3934/3934; clang-format clean.

